  "label": "TMUA early specimen · Paper 1 · Question 3",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E"
  ],
  "correct": "B",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use the meaning of a bisector",
      "body": "<p>Every point on a perpendicular bisector is equally distant from the two endpoints. An unknown point on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>-axis can be written <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Equidistance turns a perpendicular-bisector condition into a distance equation.",
      "pitfall": "The point is on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>-axis, so its second coordinate is zero.",
      "pause": "Write the two squared distances from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l01",
          "reminder": "Use both coordinate differences when forming the squared distance between points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Equate squared distances",
      "body": "<p>The distances give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></math>. Squared distances are equal whenever the nonnegative distances are equal.</p>",
      "recap": "Square both distances to avoid unnecessary square roots.",
      "pitfall": "Keep the vertical differences: the endpoints are not on the axis.",
      "pause": "Expand and cancel the matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> terms.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l01",
          "reminder": "Square both distances to avoid unnecessary square roots."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Write the intersection point as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Equidistance from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>6</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>Expand: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>41</mn></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer B.</strong> Alternatively, the midpoint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the perpendicular gradient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>; substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in the line through that midpoint gives the same result.</p>",
